=== layout: subsection

# 33. Patents, Instruments, and Data

=== layout: bookend

# 33. Questions

@body

- Does patenting an apparatus, like a new mass spectrometer, grant ownership over the data it produces?
- If a researcher patents specific equipment, does she own all data generated by that equipment?
- How did Diamond v. Diehr (1981) influence the limits of patentable subject matter, particularly regarding physical processes that use software or generate data?
- What distinctions exist between physical inventions and the data they produce?
- How does the Bayh-Dole Act facilitate the commercialization of federally funded research?
- What responsibilities do research institutions have under this act, and how do they balance public interest with commercial incentives?
- What ethical issues arise from patenting technologies critical to public health, such as a vaccine delivery system?
- Can patents on essential health technologies impact data accessibility for researchers and public health agencies?

=== layout: two-panels

# 33. Instrument Rights and Results

@left

- A patent on a mass spectrometer or RT-qPCR apparatus concerns the claimed invention.
- It does not automatically transfer ownership of every user's measurements to the patent owner.
- Contracts, employment agreements, trade secrets, privacy obligations, and database or software rights can create separate access and use questions.
- Identify the claimed right before treating data as either universally private or universally free.

@right

![33. Instrument Rights and Results](assets/set6_sources/experiment_measurement_cycle.png)

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/web/offices/pac/mpep/s2106.html

=== layout: one-panel

# 33. Software in a Physical Process

@body

- Diamond v. Diehr concerned a claimed rubber-curing process using a mathematical calculation and a computer.
- The Court evaluated the claimed process as a whole; it did not grant ownership of the mathematical relationship itself.
- Modern eligibility analysis also includes later decisions and current guidance.
- A patent on an instrument or process should not be confused with copyright in software or ownership of facts generated by its use.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/web/offices/pac/mpep/s2106.html

=== layout: one-panel

# 33. Public Funding and Patents

@body

- Bayh-Dole allows qualifying recipients to retain rights in certain inventions arising from federally funded work, subject to statutory obligations.
- Disclosure, government rights, and conditions on commercialization matter.
- This is a framework for inventions, not a rule that every item of grant-funded data becomes privately owned by an instrument manufacturer.
- Discuss public benefit, access, incentives, and accountability without assuming that one legal tool resolves them all.

@citation

https://www.grants.nih.gov/policy-and-compliance/policy-topics/intellectual-property

=== layout: subsection

# 34. Right to Repair

=== layout: bookend

# 34. Questions

@body

- What is the "Right to Repair" movement, and what are its primary objectives?
- Explain the environmental, economic, and user-rights aspects.
- How has iFixit contributed to the movement?
- Discuss iFixit's advocacy for public awareness and access to repair guides, as well as its role in legal reform efforts.
- What are examples of opposition from major companies?
- What roles did Apple iDevices and John Deere tractors play in the right to repair?
- What are the arguments against "Right to Repair" laws, and how do proponents address these concerns?
- In what ways might the right to repair conflict with safety and quality control measures in biotechnological equipment?
- Could "Right to Repair" laws influence the design and sustainability of biotechnological devices?
- What does the recent California Right to Repair bill mean for the rest of the USA?
- Why did Apple support the California "Right to Repair" bill after opposing similar legislation in the past?

=== layout: two-panels

# 34. Who Can Repair the Equipment?

@right

- Repair restrictions can involve access to\
  parts, manuals, diagnostic software, tools, pairing systems, and replacement components.
- Repair advocates emphasize cost, useful life, waste reduction, and the ability to maintain purchased products.
- Manufacturers raise issues including safety, cybersecurity, quality, and intellectual property.
- Ask which restriction addresses a demonstrated risk and whether a less restrictive approach can meet the same need.

@left

![34. Who Can Repair the Equipment?](assets/set6_sources/ifixit_logo.png)

@citation

FTC, Nixing the Fix: An FTC Report to Congress on Repair Restrictions (May 2021).

=== layout: two-panels

# 34. A Tractor Is Also a Computer

@left

- Apple devices and John Deere equipment illustrate how software and digital access can affect maintenance of a physical product.
- iFixit contributes repair information and advocacy, but an advocacy source is not the text of every applicable law.
- State repair laws differ in covered products, dates, duties, and exclusions.
- California's electronics law does not by itself establish a universal right to repair every biotechnology instrument.

@right

![34. A Tractor Is Also a Computer](assets/set6_sources/smartphones.png)

@citation

FTC, Nixing the Fix: An FTC Report to Congress on Repair Restrictions (May 2021).

=== layout: one-panel

# 34. Repairing a Biotechnology Instrument

@body

- Replacing a broken latch, servicing a pump, and changing a clinical analyzer's measurement software create different validation questions.
- After repair, a laboratory may need calibration, performance verification, contamination checks, or other documented evidence appropriate to its use.
- Safety and repairability can both be design goals.
- For your company, ask how a customer will maintain the product and demonstrate that it still works correctly.

@citation

FTC, Nixing the Fix: An FTC Report to Congress on Repair Restrictions (May 2021).

=== layout: subsection

# 35. Copyleft and the Public Domain

=== layout: bookend

# 35. Questions

@body

- What is the public domain, What will be released to the public domain this coming January 1st?
- How does copyleft differ from traditional copyright?
- How do licenses like GNU Public License (GPL) and Creative Commons (CC) work?
- Discuss the goals of copyleft?
- Explore the different types of Creative Commons licenses, including CC BY, CC BY-SA, CC BY-NC, and CC BY-NC-SA.
- How do copyleft and public domain licensing promote innovation?
- Examine how copyleft and public domain licensing foster collaboration and accelerate advancements, particularly in biotechnology, bioinformatics, and related educational materials. How does freely accessible educational materials in advancing scientific knowledge and supporting equitable access to information?
- How are textbook publishers generating profits from free online textbooks licensed under CC BY-SA by selling them as physical copies?
- To what extent might these practices be seen as "tricking" students into paying significant markups for material that is freely available online?
- Which license would you use if publishing on YouTube?
- Discuss scenarios where different licenses, such as CC BY or CC BY-NC-SA, might be appropriate.

=== layout: one-panel

# 35. Permission and Copyright Status

@body

- A copyrighted work can be shared under a license that grants specific permissions with conditions.
- Copyleft generally requires specified freedoms or licensing terms to continue with covered derivatives or distributions.
- Public-domain material is not subject to copyright restriction in the relevant jurisdiction, though other rights can still matter.
- Creative Commons licenses are not all copyleft, and free to read does not mean free of conditions.

@citation

https://creativecommons.org/cc-licenses/

=== layout: one-panel

# 35. Read the Letters on a CC License

@body

- BY requires attribution. SA adds share-alike conditions. NC limits commercial use. ND restricts sharing adaptations under the license.
- CC BY and CC BY-SA permit commercial reuse under their conditions. CC BY-NC and CC BY-NC-SA do not provide the same permission.
- CC0 is a public-domain dedication tool. The GPL is a software license with its own distribution obligations.
- Choose a license for the material and the sharing behavior you actually want.

@citation

https://creativecommons.org/cc-licenses/

=== layout: one-panel

# 35. Selling a Printed Copy Can Be Allowed

@body

- Charging for a print edition of a CC BY-SA work can be lawful when the license conditions are satisfied.
- The ethical question about misleading students is separate from whether commercial distribution is permitted.
- Attribution, license notices, and share-alike obligations\
  still matter. A buyer may value printing,\
  binding, or convenience.
- Ask whether the seller accurately represents the source and whether students know the free digital option exists.

@citation

https://creativecommons.org/cc-licenses/

=== layout: one-panel

# 35. Which January 1?

@body

- For this November 2026 lecture, the coming January 1 is January 1, 2027.
- Many qualifying U.S. works published in 1931 reach the end of a 95-year term then. Sound recordings and other categories can follow different rules.
- Copyright term depends on the type of work, dates, and jurisdiction; do not subtract one number for everything.
- For a video, also check the platform's offered license choices. YouTube's standard license and its CC BY option are not a menu of every possible CC license.

@citation

https://creativecommons.org/cc-licenses/

=== layout: one-panel

# 35. Choose the Permission You Mean

@body

{table-style=comparison}
| License or Tool | Commercial Reuse? | A Main Condition |
| --- | --- | --- |
| CC BY | Yes | Attribution |
| CC BY-SA | Yes | Attribution and share alike |
| CC BY-NC | Not granted by this license | Attribution; noncommercial use |
| CC BY-NC-SA | Not granted by this license | Attribution; noncommercial; share alike |
| CC0 | Copyright restrictions waived as far as possible | Other rights may still matter |

These are shorthand comparisons; read the actual license for a real reuse.

@citation

https://creativecommons.org/cc-licenses/

